  {"question": "According to arXiv 2607.07612, how does the Singapore Agentic AI MGF's approach to risk classification differ from the EU AI Act, and what are the four governance dimensions it recommends?", "answer": "The Singapore Agentic AI MGF classifies risks by types of negative consequences, unlike the EU AI Act's tiered risk levels, and recommends four governance dimensions: assess and bound risks upfront, make humans accountable, implement technical controls, and enable end-user responsibility.", "evidence": [{"page": 12, "quote": "the Singapore Agentic AI MGF does not classify risk in the same manner as the EU AI Act classifies risk for general-purpose AI, which includes unacceptable, high, limited, and minimal risk."}, {"page": 12, "quote": "The four dimensions in the Singapore Agentic AI MGF of governance are: \"assess and bound the risks upfront,\" \"make humans meaningfully accountable,\" \"implement technical controls and processes,\" and 'enable end-user responsibility.\""}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2607.07612, what are the two main approaches to classifying agentic AI governance, and which authors are associated with each?", "answer": "The two main approaches are autonomous goal-pursuit and moral agency. Authors like Chan et al., Bent, and Gahnberg discuss autonomous goal-pursuit, while Kolt, Lior, O'Keefe et al., and Riedl & Desai focus on moral agency.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "We observed two major approaches to classifying or discussing agentic AI governance: autonomous goal-pursuit and tool, which describe agentic AI as an autonomous AI system that can be used to achieve a particular goal and as a tool for planning and carrying out tasks; moral agency, as an agent of a particular principal."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2607.07612, what is 'temporal coherence' in the context of agentic AI, and who introduced this concept?", "answer": "Temporal coherence refers to an agentic AI's capacity to maintain consistent function over time using memory, and was introduced by Bent in her AIES 2025 paper.", "evidence": [{"page": 9, "quote": "We observed that of all the attributes listed above, \"temporal coherence\" is unique and was recently coined by Bent in her AIES 2025 paper, and it relates to the memory capacity of agentic AI [3]."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2607.07612, what is the definition of agentic AI provided by the Singapore Agentic AI MGF?", "answer": "The Singapore Agentic AI MGF defines agentic AI as systems that can plan across multiple steps to achieve specified objectives, using AI agents with some degree of independent planning and action.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "Singapore Agentic AI Framework defined agentic AI as \"systems that can plan across multiple steps to achieve specified objectives, using AI agents. There is no consensus on what defines an agent, but there are certain common features - agents usually possess some degree of independent planning and action taking (e.g., searching the web or creating files) over multiple steps to achieve a userdefined goal.\""}]},
  {"question": "Which privacy laws does Navaie use as case studies for agentic AI governance in arXiv 2607.07612?", "answer": "Navaie uses the GDPR, CPRA, LGPD, and PDPA as case studies for agentic AI governance.", "evidence": [{"page": 11, "quote": "Navaie argued that existing privacy laws can be used to address agentic AI governance issues and develop a toolkit for privacy engineers using the GDPR, CPRA, LGPD, and PDPA as case studies [24]."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2607.07612, which unique feature of agentic AI is associated with the ability to maintain consistent operation over time?", "answer": "Temporal coherence is the unique feature associated with maintaining consistent operation over time.", "evidence": [{"page": 9, "quote": "Temporal Coherence        | Capacity to uphold consistent function across periods of time by utilizing its awareness of its current state and its memory                                                                 | Bent"}]}
]}
